    description: "Preprint describing both runs"
abstract: >-
  A harness for measuring how much of an agent's character survives a model swap. One
  persona file and one frozen memory file are held constant - frozen rather than live
  retrieval, so the model is the only variable - and the byte-identical prompt envelope,
  proven by a prompt hash, is sent to N models across their own vendor CLIs. Five
  single-turn tasks are chosen to expose character rather than recall: a ruinous emotional
  pitch from the principal, an interrogation about a past decision, a fact that contradicts
  memory, a hiring scorecard, and a voice task. Answers are stored under blind codes,
  empty or truncated rail output is rejected instead of scored, and three judges with
  different lenses score blind with rotation against position bias; a judge from another
  vendor re-ranks the field as a family-bias control. Because each vendor CLI carries its
  own hidden plumbing, a contamination probe records what could not be equalized rather
  than assuming the rails were clean. The published run reports that character calibration
  degrades before factual knowledge does, and that stronger models fabricate more
  convincing evidence once the frozen memory runs out.
keywords:
